//? Title: BKM-Style Logarithms and Exponentials: Factors, Truncation and Roundoff
//? Subtitle: A checked real greedy variant, actual iteration traces and the ln(3) plateau
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Approximate a natural logarithm or exponential by selecting small factors from
a fixed table, while making the accepted and rejected choices visible. The
restricted real algorithm in datamunge pairs a running product with a sum of
logarithms. We retain the original numerical examples, derive a truncation
envelope, and distinguish that exact-arithmetic argument from double rounding.
The original ln(3) example reaches its product after two attempts; it does not
gain another correct bit at every subsequent iteration.
)

\toc

> The method

Let \(a_k=\log(1+2^{-k})\). Start with product x=1 and log sum s=0.
At shift k, form the two candidates

$$
x'=x+2^{-k}x,\qquad s'=s+a_k.
$$
\alttext(The product candidate is x plus x scaled by two to minus k. The logarithm candidate is s plus the logarithm of one plus two to minus k.)

For ln(v), accept the pair when x' is at most v. For exp(y), accept it when
s' is at most y. Otherwise keep both state variables. In exact arithmetic,
\(s=\log x\) is invariant because each accepted multiplication adds the
logarithm of precisely that factor. Every k is an attempted digit, including
rejections. Both interfaces and their traces use the same native loop.

The original BKM paper treats complex and signed digits. This report follows
the repository's narrower real variant with digits zero or one and k=0,...,52.
There is no range reduction: ln inputs must lie between 1 and the all-factors
product, while exp inputs lie between 0 and the all-entries sum. The library
computes those actual double endpoints and rejects nonfinite or out-of-range
inputs instead of silently saturating.

Scaling by \(2^{-k}\) suggests shifts in fixed-point hardware. Here the code
uses double `std::ldexp` and additions, and generates the table with platform
`std::log1p` once. This is an inspectable numerical implementation; no hardware
throughput or correctly rounded elementary-function claim follows from it.

> Simple example: ln(3)

At k=0 the product becomes 2, adding ln(2). At k=1 it becomes 3, adding
ln(1.5). Their exact sum is ln(3). The next candidate is 3.75 and is rejected.
Every later candidate is also above 3, so the running product stays at 3.

```{cpp, file="figures/bkm_factors.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bkm_ln_trace(3,3);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.9;style.height=1.4;
  diagram.node("initial",{0,0},"Initial\nx = 1\ns = 0",style);
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<trace.steps.size();++i) {
    const auto& step=trace.steps[i];
    style.fill=step.accepted?dm::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::RGB{255,237,213};
    const auto id=std::format("step{}",i);
    diagram.node(id,{5.*(i+1),0},std::format("After k = {}\nx = {}\ns = {:.6f}",step.shift,step.product,step.log_sum),style)
           .edge(i?std::format("step{}",i-1):"initial",id,step.accepted?"accept":"reject");
  }
  diagram.text({7.5,2},"ln(3): accept factor 2, accept factor 1.5, reject factor 1.25")
         .text({7.5,-2},"The product reaches 3; the rounded log sum can differ from std::log(3).");
  diagram.save_svg("build/bkm_factors.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/bkm_factors.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The labels and decisions come from the canonical three-attempt trace. The rejected candidate is x'=3.75, while the recorded state remains x=3.)
\alttext(Four boxes show initial product one, accepted product two, accepted product three, and unchanged product three after rejection. The logarithm sum changes on the first two edges only.)

> Original examples and the finite domain

The following preserves all five logarithm and four exponential arguments
from the original report. Standard-library discrepancies are numerical checks,
not certificates of exact error; the independent development oracle below uses
100-digit decimal arithmetic as a further control.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto domain=dm::algorithms::bkm_domain();
  std::print("Supported ln interval: [1, {:.17g}]; exp interval: [0, {:.17g}]; terms: {}.\n\n",
             domain.max_argument,domain.max_exponent,domain.terms);
  std::print("| function | argument | native result | standard library | absolute discrepancy |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (double v:{1.5,2.,2.718281828,3.,4.5})
    std::print("| ln | {:.9f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.3e} |\n",v,
               dm::algorithms::bkm_ln(v),std::log(v),std::abs(dm::algorithms::bkm_ln(v)-std::log(v)));
  for (double y:{.25,.5,1.,1.5})
    std::print("| exp | {:.9f} | {:.12f} | {:.12f} | {:.3e} |\n",y,
               dm::algorithms::bkm_exp(y),std::exp(y),std::abs(dm::algorithms::bkm_exp(y)-std::exp(y)));
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bkm_ln_trace(3);
  const auto accepted=std::count_if(trace.steps.begin(),trace.steps.end(),[](const auto& step){return step.accepted;});
  std::print("\nln(3): {} accepted factors out of {} attempts; final product {}.\n",
             accepted,trace.steps.size(),trace.steps.back().product);
}
```
