> Checking the theory: what one more attempt can guarantee

With infinitely many exact factors, the remaining logarithm capacity after N
attempts is

$$
T_N=\sum_{k=N}^{\infty}\log(1+2^{-k})
<\sum_{k=N}^{\infty}2^{-k}=2^{1-N}.
$$
\alttext(The sum of all remaining log factors is less than two to the power one minus N.)

The remaining capacity covers the current digit: indeed
\(\prod_{j>k}(1+2^{-j})>1+\sum_{j>k}2^{-j}=1+2^{-k}\),
so \(a_k<T_{k+1}\). Initially the residual is within the whole capacity.
If the greedy step accepts a_k, the residual falls into the next capacity;
if it rejects, the residual is below a_k and hence also below that capacity.
Induction gives a nonnegative log residual r_N below T_N. The finite 53-factor
input interval is contained in this infinite-factor interval, so the same
exact-arithmetic bound applies to its prefixes.

For logarithms, r_N is ln(v)-s_N. For exponentials it is y-ln(x_N), giving
\(0\le1-x_N/e^y=1-e^{-r_N}\le r_N<T_N\).
Thus worst-case truncation shrinks geometrically. This does not say that
every accepted digit, or every attempt, improves the observed error by one bit.
Exact factorisation can finish early, while rounded table entries, sums and
products eventually dominate the tail.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bkm_convergence.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <numbers>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> terms,bound,pi_terms,pi_bits,three_terms,three_bits;
  unsigned zero_discrepancies=0;
  for (int n=1;n<=53;++n) {
    terms.push_back(n);bound.push_back(n-1);
    const double pi_error=std::abs(dm::algorithms::bkm_ln_trace(std::numbers::pi,n).value-std::log(std::numbers::pi));
    const double three_error=std::abs(dm::algorithms::bkm_ln_trace(3,n).value-std::log(3.));
    if (pi_error>0) {pi_terms.push_back(n);pi_bits.push_back(-std::log2(pi_error));}
    else ++zero_discrepancies;
    if (three_error>0) {three_terms.push_back(n);three_bits.push_back(-std::log2(three_error));}
    else ++zero_discrepancies;
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(pi_terms,pi_bits,"ln(pi)",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .points(three_terms,three_bits,"ln(3)",dm::RGB{217,119,6},4)
      .lines(terms,bound,"exact tail bound",dm::RGB{148,163,184},2)
      .title("Truncation envelope and the double plateau")
      .x_label("attempted shifts N").y_label("-log2 absolute discrepancy")
      .y_limits(0,66).size(900,480).save_svg("build/bkm_convergence.svg");
  std::print("Zero standard-library discrepancies omitted from the accuracy metric: {}.\n\n",zero_discrepancies);
  std::print("| N | ln(pi) absolute discrepancy | exp(1) relative discrepancy | exact tail envelope |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int n:{1,2,4,8,16,32,48,53})
    std::print("| {} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} |\n",n,
        std::abs(dm::algorithms::bkm_ln_trace(std::numbers::pi,n).value-std::log(std::numbers::pi)),
        std::abs(dm::algorithms::bkm_exp_trace(1,n).value/std::exp(1.)-1),std::ldexp(1.,1-n));
}
```
