\caption(The sweep checks both functions across their supported unreduced intervals. It measures numerical discrepancies, not running time or hardware cost.)

Native tests add 10,001-point long-double reference sweeps, every prefix length,
trace replay, adjacent representable domain boundaries and nonfinite-input
rejection. A separate development check reconstructs accepted products exactly
and logarithm sums at 100 decimal digits, distinguishing finite-factor
truncation from the implementation's rounding. None of these comparisons
establish correct rounding for every double input.

> Conclusion

The shared core now supplies checked scalar functions and actual candidate,
acceptance, product and log-sum traces. The original examples retain their
numerical agreement, while ln(3) correctly illustrates early factor completion
and a rounding plateau. The geometric tail bound describes exact truncation;
the measured double discrepancies expose its practical precision limit.

Each scalar call attempts 53 factors with constant auxiliary storage after table
initialisation. Recording N attempts uses O(N) owned storage. General real
arguments require a separately designed range reduction, and the full complex
or signed-digit BKM construction is outside this API. Generated inputs suffice
for these arithmetic properties; no observed dataset is needed.

> References

- Bajard, J.-C., Kla, S. and Muller, J.-M. (1994). *BKM: A New Hardware Algorithm for Complex Elementary Functions*. IEEE Transactions on Computers, 43(8), 955–963. https://doi.org/10.1109/12.295857. Author's copy: https://perso.ens-lyon.fr/jean-michel.muller/BKM94.pdf. The restricted real greedy variant and its finite-double limits are analysed here separately from that full algorithm.
